title The pivot does not move
prompt Keaton -- how about hinge or separator or something similar, for the word flap
think Measured both against the tree before answering, since a naming ruling made from memory is the one that collides later.
obs SEPARATOR IS SPOKEN FOR, 272 tracked uses, and one of them is a literal row in ascii-first.md's own substitution table -- middle dot separator. It also describes no part of the fault: a separator divides two things that should be apart, and this fault is two answers about one thing.
obs HINGE CARRIES THE ORDINARY SENSE ALREADY, 74 tracked uses including hinges-on meaning depends-on, which is positive and load-bearing where this word must name a defect.
obs THE DECIDING REASON IS SEMANTIC RATHER THAN THE COUNTS. A hinge is the part that does NOT move -- it is the fixed pivot a flap swings on -- so naming the fault hinge names the axis instead of the motion, and the motion is the whole fault.
obs Checked the rest of the physical family. flutter is CLEAN at 0 tracked hits and is a real engineering term, yet it means rapid continuous oscillation where this fault is intermittent: %700 read one red in fifteen.
obs Recommended keeping flap on a stated test rather than on incumbency: the fault is an instrument whose reading depends on something other than its subject, recognised by giving TWO ANSWERS, and flap is the only candidate carrying the two-ness and the motion in one syllable.
obs Offered two-answered as the newcomer-clarity option, strongest exactly where flap is weakest, and contended as the precise diagnosis for the race-shaped flaps -- which belongs inside a row rather than as the class name.
loom naming candidate=separator tracked=272 candidate=hinge tracked=74 candidate=chatter tracked=142 candidate=flutter tracked=0 candidate=waver tracked=1 candidate=shimmer tracked=5
loom flap tracked_uses=122
